; tlo.macro - reference the tool ALREADY IN THE SPINDLE against the toolsetter puck.
;
; Extracted from tc.macro on 2026-09-14, unchanged in behaviour, because a SECOND copy of this
; sequence had grown in the sender (StartJobView.EmitTloReference, emitted inline into generated
; g-code) and had already drifted from it in three ways:
;
;   - it probed "G38.2 Z-80" where this had been raised to -90 after a short V-bit threw Alarm:5
;     five mm short of the puck on real hardware, 2026-07-27;
;   - it restored a hardcoded G54 where this restores the CALLER'S WCS, which tc.macro's own comment
;     says explicitly must not be hardcoded;
;   - it selected the toolsetter input unconditionally where this branches three ways.
;
; The last two could never have been right in a streamed program: they need o-word branching, and
; o-word flow control cannot be streamed to grblHAL - a bare ELSE/ENDIF is dropped by the line
; pipeline and leaves the controller's o-word engine waiting for an ENDIF that never arrives. So the
; copy was not merely at risk of drifting, it was structurally incapable of matching. That is the
; reason this lives on the controller and is CALLED, exactly as pcorner.macro is.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Inputs - globals set before a NO-ARG call (the pcorner convention: grblHAL's O-word CALL does not
; take multiple bracketed args reliably, it parses the extra brackets as g-code):
;
;   #<_tlo_toolid>     tool in the spindle. 8 = the 3D probe stylus, which is sensitive and must not
;                      bear down on the puck, so it probes the MAIN input and triggers on first
;                      touch. Anything else = a rigid cutting tool with no electrical sensing, which
;                      pushes the puck's own NC switch on the TOOLSETTER input.
;   #<_tc_touchplate>  1 = no toolsetter hardware exists; every tool probes the main input. Set by
;                      tc.macro's own header; read here so both callers see one rule.
;   #<_tlo_ref>        the machine-wide baseline. 0 (or unset) = this tool BECOMES the reference and
;                      no G43.1 is applied. Non-zero = apply G43.1 relative to it.
;
; Outputs:
;   #<_probe_z>        the probed machine Z of this tool against the puck.
;   G43.1 applied (or #<_tlo_ref> set), and $TLR sent either way.
;
; Leaves the machine AT the puck, lifted 10mm. The caller owns getting somewhere useful afterwards -
; tc.macro returns to G30; a sender-generated program does whatever it needs next.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(PRINT, TLO01 ENTER tool=#<_tlo_toolid> ref=#<_tlo_ref> touchplate=#<_tc_touchplate>)

; Save the CALLER's active WCS before selecting G59.3 below - restored (never hardcoded to G54) at the
; end, so a tool referenced mid-program under any other WCS comes back to the RIGHT origin. #5220 is
; grblHAL's own active-coordinate-system parameter (1=G54 .. 6=G59, 7=G59.1, 8=G59.2, 9=G59.3).
; Confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-27: hardcoding G54 on return left Pocket's finishing pass running
; under the wrong WCS after its own mid-program tool change - its first move, computed in G59 and
; executed under G54, landed outside travel and hit a soft limit.
#<_tlo_wcs> = #5220

; Suspend any active G92 for the duration, restored at the end. WCO = G5x + G92 + TLO, so a leftover
; G92 shifts every non-G53 move here - including the "G0 Z0" over the puck, which is NOT a machine
; move however much it reads like one. Confirmed on real hardware 2026-08-16: a persistent G92 of
; Z+37 made that target machine Z+37 against an envelope topping out at 0 -> ALARM:2 before the probe.
; G92.2/G92.3 (not G92.1): this runs mid-job and a job may legitimately be working under a G92;
; destroying it would move every remaining cut. Idempotent, so a caller that already suspended its
; own G92 loses nothing by this pair.
; WARNING: G92.3 restores from NVS, so it depends on G92 persistence being ENABLED ($384=0, the
; default and this machine's setting). With $384=1 revisit this pair.
G92.2

; --- Rapid to G59.3 (over the puck) ---
; Safe-Z goto rather than a coordinated "G0 X0 Y0 Z0", which would move Z while traversing.
; Traversing at machine -5 does not rely on G59.3's own Z being set high enough to clear the puck.
G53 G0 Z-5
G59.3
G0 X0 Y0
G0 Z0
(PRINT, TLO02 at G59.3)

; --- Select the probe input for what is actually in the spindle ---
; G65 P5 Q<id>: Q0 = Probe_Default (main), Q1 = Probe_Toolsetter (probe.h:40).
o150 IF [#<_tc_touchplate> EQ 1]
  G65 P5 Q0
  (PRINT, TLO03 main probe selected (touch-plate mode))
o150 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_toolid> EQ 8]
  G65 P5 Q0
  (PRINT, TLO03 main probe selected for T8)
o150 ELSE
  G65 P5 Q1
  (PRINT, TLO03 toolsetter selected for T#<_tlo_toolid>)
o150 ENDIF

G91
; -90mm (was -80mm): a short V-bit (chamfer) sits well above the puck at G59.3's park height, which is
; calibrated for the LONGEST tool in use - confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-27, Alarm 5 (probe fail,
; no contact within programmed travel) landing 5mm short of the puck for the V-bit specifically.
G38.2 Z-90 F500
G0 Z2
G38.2 Z-5 F25
#<_probe_z> = #5063
G0 Z10
G90
G65 P5 Q0

; Restore the caller's own WCS - see #<_tlo_wcs> above for why this must NOT be a hardcoded G54.
o120 IF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 1]
  G54
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 2]
  G55
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 3]
  G56
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 4]
  G57
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 5]
  G58
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 6]
  G59
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 7]
  G59.1
o120 ELSEIF [#<_tlo_wcs> EQ 8]
  G59.2
o120 ELSE
  G59.3
o120 ENDIF
; The caller's G92 offset back, alongside the WCS just restored - the two together are the coordinate
; frame this macro borrowed, and they are handed back as a pair.
G92.3
(PRINT, TLO04 probe_z=#<_probe_z>)

o199 IF [EXISTS[#<_tlo_ref>]]
o199 ELSE
  #<_tlo_ref> = 0
o199 ENDIF
o200 IF [#<_tlo_ref> EQ 0]
  #<_tlo_ref> = #<_probe_z>
  (PRINT, TLO05 TLO REF SET ref=#<_tlo_ref>)
o200 ELSE
  G43.1 Z[#<_probe_z> - #<_tlo_ref>]
  (PRINT, TLO06 TLO APPLIED tlo=[#<_probe_z> - #<_tlo_ref>])
o200 ENDIF

; $TLR - the REAL grblHAL system command that commits the tool length reference to the controller's
; own native TLR flag (GrblViewModel.IsTloReferenceSet / the status report's TLR: field), the same one
; the Probing tab's Tool Length flow uses. #<_tlo_ref> above is a SEPARATE, older, script-only
; convention that never touched the native flag - confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-27: every PREREQ
; gate checking "tlo" could never pass even after a successful reference.
; Sent EVERY time, both branches above, not just the first-ever reference: a caller's G49 (tc.macro
; cancels the old tool's TLO before probing the new one) clears the native flag as a side effect on
; every real tool swap, so a job with multiple tool changes would otherwise see TLR go false after the
; FIRST swap and never come back - confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-27.
$TLR
(PRINT, TLO07 EXIT)
